Install moode using RPI image builder

Install ddcutil





Install uart5_listener


We are using Uart 5 , so make sure that the configuration file /boot/firmware/config.txt

contains the line :: 
    dtoverlay=uart5



Install the PI serial Library ::

    sudo python3 -m pip install pyserial

Validate it was install correctly
    python3 -c "import serial; print(serial.__version__)"


If 'PIP' is missing then install  it

    sudo apt install python3-pip
and re-install pyserial

sudo python3 -m pip install pyserial


Create Service file :: 
    Create File : sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/uart_listener.service


if you get an error 'sudo python3 -m pip install pyserial error: externally-managed-environment'

then try this command :

sudo apt install python3-serial

Add the following text :

[Unit]
Description=UART5 Listener for ESP32
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=simple
ExecStart=/usr/bin/python3 /home/antho/uart5_listener.py
Restart=always
User=antho
Group=antho
WorkingDirectory=/home/antho
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target


------------------------

Create the uart python file  :
    nano /home/antho/uart5_listener.py

Add the code from the file UArt5Listener.py and save

change the Access settings

    chmod +x /home/antho/uart5_listener.py

make sure that the user has access :

    sudo chown antho:antho /home/antho/uart5_listener.py
    sudo chmod +x /home/antho/uart5_listener.py


Once installed Execute the following command to Start the service and return the status

        sudo systemctl daemon-reload
        sudo systemctl enable uart5_listener.service
        sudo systemctl start uart5_listener.service
        sudo systemctl status uart5_listener.service

Check the logs to confirm :

journalctl -u uart5_listener.service -f
